#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gnss-probe - find out what the GNSS receiver on this node actually is.

  sudo gnss-probe                      probe the device from /etc/default/gpsd
  sudo gnss-probe /dev/ttyS0           probe a specific device
  sudo gnss-probe --json out.json      also write machine-readable results
  gnss-probe --selftest                run the built-in unit tests, touch no hardware

Read-only. It sends nothing but polls, and it cannot send anything else -- see
"The read-only guarantee" below.


--- why this exists ---------------------------------------------------------

Our own docs disagree about what the receiver on the Waveshare LoRaWAN/GNSS HAT
is. The gpsd design spec and most of the linux README call it a Quectel L76K;
gpsd-gnss-tuning.conf and gnss-set-baud call it an Allystar URANUS5. Those are
different parts with different binary protocols, and the $PCAS command set we
already depend on does not settle it -- $PCAS is used by both Allystar and CASIC
parts.

Three protocols are therefore in play, and the framing byte pair tells them
apart:

    u-blox      b5 62      <- what the HAT's receiver emits
    Allystar    f1 d9      <- gpsd 3.26's driver_allystar.c; ignored completely
    CASIC       ba ce      <- never emitted, but what it actually answers to

That matters beyond curiosity. gpsd's Allystar driver shows that the b5 62
messages this receiver answers are a deliberately partial u-blox *emulation*:
CFG-PRT, CFG-NAV5, CFG-GNSS, CFG-SBAS, CFG-RATE and MON-VER all NAK over b5 62
because their real implementations live at f1 d9 with Allystar payloads. If this
chip speaks f1 d9, a pile of things we documented as "not supported" are simply
things we asked for in the wrong protocol -- including the constellation mask
(is Galileo available?) and the PPS configuration.

This tool answers "which protocol, and which of those messages exist" without
guessing, and without writing anything to the receiver.


--- the read-only guarantee -------------------------------------------------

There is no send(class, id, payload) anywhere in this file. The only transmit
path is Prober.poll(n), which takes an index into the frozen POLLS table and
cannot express a frame outside it. That is a structural property, not a promise
to be careful, which is why --selftest can meaningfully assert it.

A blanket "empty payload means it is a poll" rule would NOT be safe: the per-port
CFG-PRT poll legitimately carries a one-byte port ID (driver_allystar.c:1838).
Hence an explicit table rather than a length check.


--- what it does ------------------------------------------------------------

Phase 1 (passive, sends nothing). Read the raw device for a few seconds and
classify what is already on the wire: which of the three sync patterns appear,
and which NMEA sentences the *receiver itself* emits.

That second part is worth having on its own. gpsd synthesizes NMEA for its
clients from binary data, so sentences the receiver never sends still show up
downstream -- the linux README warns about exactly this trap, and several of our
claims about this receiver's native sentence set sit on the far side of that
warning. Phase 1 reads the device directly, so it settles them.

Phase 2 (active). Send a version poll in each transmittable framing, see which
draws a reply, then sweep the POLLS table in whichever framing won.

All three framings transmit. CASIC was detect-only in the first version of this
tool, on the grounds that its layout was undocumented and a transmitter that
could not be verified might produce wrong conclusions. That caution was right
in principle and wrong in effect: it meant the tool could not ask the question
that turned out to matter. The Quectel L76K GNSS Protocol Specification V1.1
section 3.1 documents the layout, and its worked examples are used as test
vectors in --selftest, so the encoder is checked against vendor bytes rather
than against a guess.

The finding that justified it: on the receiver this was written for, the chip
EMITS b5 62 and ACCEPTS ba ce. Sweeping only the framing seen on the wire finds
nothing but refusals. What a receiver emits and what it parses are separate
questions and this tool must ask both.

On naming: that receiver answers $PCAS06 with "SW=URANUS5,V5.3.0.0". SW is the
*software* field -- the L76K spec's own example is "MA=CASIC" for manufacturer
-- so URANUS5 is a firmware string and does not identify the vendor. Everything
checkable (CASIC framing, NMEA talker IDs, GSA SystemID values, satellite ID
ranges) matches the CASIC part that spec documents. Do not read a version
string as a part number; that mistake was made here once already.


--- notes for whoever runs this next ----------------------------------------

* An ACK-NAK is a RESULT, not a failure. "this chip refuses CFG-SURVEY" is one
  of the answers we are paying for.

* The sweep is paced ~250 ms per probe on purpose. driver_allystar.c:1800 warns
  that "some parts get cranky when they get too many configuration changes at
  once", and spaces its own polls four parse-cycles apart. Rushing risks reading
  a chip that has fallen behind as "unsupported", which would poison every
  result at once.

* Both gpsd.socket AND gpsd.service get stopped. Stopping the service alone is
  not enough: gpsd is socket-activated, and meshcored reconnects to it with
  backoff, so it would respawn gpsd mid-probe and take the port back.

* Whatever speed this leaves the port at does not need restoring. gpsd's
  ExecStartPre runs gnss-set-baud and its ExecStartPost re-enables GSV/GSA, so
  restarting gpsd heals the port by construction.

* Restarting gpsd costs the stratum-1 clock for a couple of minutes while
  chrony's refclock filter refills. That is printed before anything is stopped.

Exit status: 0 probed (including when everything NAKs), 1 refused to run or
could not get the port, 2 ran but recognised no framing at either speed,
130 interrupted.
"""
